Okay, let's break down how to read a CSV file in JavaScript, assuming you're working in a browser environment (e.g., a webpage) or in a Node.js environment. I'll provide examples for both.

**Browser Environment (Webpage)**

In a browser, you can't directly access files on the user's computer without their explicit permission.  You'll typically use an `<input type="file">` element to allow the user to select a CSV file.  Then, you'll read the file using the `FileReader` API.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>CSV Reader</title>
</head>
<body>
  <input type="file" id="csvFile" accept=".csv">
  <button onclick="readFile()">Read CSV</button>
  <div id="output"></div>

  <script>
    function readFile() {
      const fileInput = document.getElementById('csvFile');
      const file = fileInput.files[0]; // Get the selected file

      if (file) {
        const reader = new FileReader();

        reader.onload = function(event) {
          const csvText = event.target.result; // The CSV data as a string
          const data = parseCSV(csvText);
          displayData(data);
        };

        reader.onerror = function(error) {
          document.getElementById('output').textContent = 'Error reading file: ' + error;
        };

        reader.readAsText(file); // Start reading the file as text
      } else {
        document.getElementById('output').textContent = 'Please select a CSV file.';
      }
    }

    function parseCSV(csvText) {
      const lines = csvText.split('\n'); // Split into lines
      const result = [];

      for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {
        const line = lines[i].trim(); // Remove leading/trailing whitespace
        if (line === "") continue; // Skip empty lines

        const values = line.split(','); // Split into values (assuming comma delimiter)
        result.push(values);
      }

      return result;
    }

    function displayData(data) {
      const outputDiv = document.getElementById('output');
      outputDiv.innerHTML = ''; // Clear previous content

      const table = document.createElement('table');
      for (let i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {
        const rowData = data[i];
        const row = document.createElement('tr');

        for (let j = 0; j < rowData.length; j++) {
          const cell = document.createElement('td');
          cell.textContent = rowData[j];
          row.appendChild(cell);
        }

        table.appendChild(row);
      }

      outputDiv.appendChild(table);
    }
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Explanation:**

1. **HTML Structure:**
   - `<input type="file" id="csvFile" accept=".csv">`: This creates a file input element.  `accept=".csv"` restricts the user to selecting CSV files.
   - `<button onclick="readFile()">Read CSV</button>`: A button that, when clicked, calls the `readFile()` JavaScript function.
   - `<div id="output"></div>`:  A `div` where the parsed CSV data will be displayed.

2. **`readFile()` Function:**
   - `const fileInput = document.getElementById('csvFile');`: Gets a reference to the file input element.
   - `const file = fileInput.files[0];`:  `fileInput.files` is a `FileList` object, which contains the files selected by the user.  `[0]` gets the first selected file.
   - `if (file)`: Checks if a file has been selected.
   - `const reader = new FileReader();`: Creates a `FileReader` object.  This object is used to read the contents of the file.
   - `reader.onload = function(event) { ... }`:  This defines a function that will be executed when the file has been successfully read.  The `event` object contains the result of the read operation.
     - `const csvText = event.target.result;`:  `event.target.result` contains the contents of the file as a string.
     - `const data = parseCSV(csvText);`: Calls the `parseCSV()` function (explained below) to convert the CSV text into a structured array of arrays (each inner array represents a row).
     - `displayData(data);`: Calls the `displayData()` function (explained below) to display the parsed data in the `output` div.
   - `reader.onerror = function(error) { ... }`: Defines a function that will be executed if an error occurs while reading the file.
   - `reader.readAsText(file);`:  This is the crucial line.  It starts the process of reading the file as text. The `onload` function will be called when this is complete.
   - `else { ... }`: Handles the case where no file was selected.

3. **`parseCSV(csvText)` Function:**
   - `const lines = csvText.split('\n');`: Splits the CSV text into an array of lines, using the newline character (`\n`) as the delimiter.
   - `const result = [];`: Initializes an empty array to store the parsed data.
   - `for (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) { ... }`: Iterates over each line.
     - `const line = lines[i].trim();`: Removes leading/trailing whitespace from the line.
     - `if (line === "") continue;`: Skips empty lines.
     - `const values = line.split(',');`: Splits the line into an array of values, using the comma (`,`) as the delimiter.  **Important:** This assumes your CSV file uses commas as delimiters.  If it uses a different delimiter (e.g., semicolon), you'll need to change this.
     - `result.push(values);`: Adds the array of values (representing a row) to the `result` array.
   - `return result;`: Returns the `result` array, which is a 2D array representing the CSV data.

4. **`displayData(data)` Function:**
   - `const outputDiv = document.getElementById('output');`: Gets a reference to the `output` div.
   - `outputDiv.innerHTML = '';`: Clears any previous content in the `output` div.
   - `const table = document.createElement('table');`: Creates a `<table>` element.
   - The nested `for` loops iterate through the `data` array (rows and columns).
